//
// The Libraries chapter's model of `use`: `pub` decides the BARE spelling, the prefix reaches
// everything.
//
// The chapter used to teach the opposite — that a library name needs the `libname::` prefix
// and that omitting it is a parse error — and every sentence saying so lived in a comment, so
// the suite ran none of them while the bare call compiled and answered.  What made the mistake
// survivable is that both spellings work for a `pub` name: a chapter can teach the long one
// exclusively and every example in it still passes.
//
// So the chapter now shows both spellings on one constant and one function, and the boundary
// they sit on is pinned here: the bare form is available for a `pub` name and refused for a
// name the library did not mark, which is the whole of what `pub` decides.  A reader who takes
// the chapter at its word and drops the prefix is relying on this.
//
// @ARGS: --lib tests/lib
//
// @falsified-at: none — a LOCK.  It records where a boundary IS rather than catching a
//   regression, so there is no build on which it fails and no `make falsify` reference to
//   compare against.
//
//   Measured by hand in both directions instead, because a guard whose point is a REFUSAL
//   passes most easily when it is proving nothing:
//     - marking `pubvis_hidden` as `pub` in the fixture makes the @EXPECT_ERROR cell compile,
//       and the file fails;
//     - dropping `pub` from `pubvis_shown` makes the bare call in the running cell unknown,
//       and the file fails;
//     - breaking either assertion in the running cell fails it too, so the file is not
//       passing on its refusal alone.
//   Restoring each returns the suite to green.
//
//   `make falsify` reads `expect 1/1` and INERT on both backends, which is the right answer
//   for a lock: the declared refusal matched, and a file that refuses on every build cannot
//   move against a control.

use pubvis::*;

// `use pubvis::*;` brings every `pub` name into this file's namespace, so both spellings reach
// it; a bare `use pubvis;` would bring in only the prefix (@C98).  The prefixed form is
// what the chapter used to teach exclusively; the bare form is what it now says also works.
fn a_pub_name_answers_to_both_spellings() {
  pv_bare = pubvis_shown(3, 4);
  pv_qualified = pubvis::pubvis_shown(3, 4);
  assert(pv_bare == 7, "a pub library fn called bare: {pv_bare}");
  assert(pv_qualified == 7, "the same fn through the prefix: {pv_qualified}");
  assert(pubvis::PUBVIS_SHOWN_LIMIT == 100, "a pub constant through the prefix");
  assert(PUBVIS_SHOWN_LIMIT == 100, "the same constant written bare");
}

// The other half of the same boundary: a definition without `pub` never entered the namespace,
// so the prefix is the only way in.  Nothing is hidden — the value is right here — but the
// short spelling is not on offer.
fn a_non_pub_name_is_reachable_only_through_the_prefix() {
  pv_hidden = pubvis::pubvis_hidden(3, 4);
  assert(pv_hidden == 7, "a non-pub library fn through the prefix: {pv_hidden}");
  assert(pubvis::PUBVIS_HIDDEN_LIMIT == 42, "a non-pub constant through the prefix");
}

// And the bare spelling of that same name is refused.  This is the cell the chapter cannot
// carry: an `assert` can never reach a program that does not compile.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Unknown function pubvis_hidden
fn the_bare_spelling_of_a_non_pub_name_is_refused() {
  pv_no = pubvis_hidden(3, 4);
  assert(pv_no == 7, "unreachable: the call above is refused");
}
